{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 30, :comments [{:quotes [{:he "לא תותירו ממנו עד בקר", :translit "lo totiru mimenu ad boker", :gloss "you shall not leave over from it until morning"} {:he "אני ה'", :translit "ani hashem", :gloss "I am YHWH"} {:he "ושמרתם מצותי", :translit "ushmartem mitzvotai", :gloss "and you shall keep My commandments"} {:he "שאין מעבירין על המצות", :translit "she'ein ma'abirin al hamitzvot", :gloss "that one does not pass over the commandments"} {:he "לא תותירו המצוות", :translit "lo totiru hamitzvot", :gloss "you shall not leave over the commandments"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :other, :note "Semikhut parshiyot (adjacency): the verse about leaving over is juxtaposed with the verse about keeping commandments"}], :frame {:text "The principle that one should not pass over a mitzvah, expressed as שאין מעבירין על המצות, is found in the Talmud (Pesachim 3a, Sukkah 25a, Yoma 33a, Bava Kamma 38a). The Ba'al HaTurim applies this hermeneutic of adjacency (semikhut parshiyot) — the fact that Leviticus 22:30 is followed immediately in Leviticus 22:31 by 'I am YHWH; and you shall keep My commandments' — to transform the plain sense of 'do not leave over [the thanksgiving offering] until morning' into a broader exhortation: do not leave commandments unfulfilled, do not pass over a mitzvah when the opportunity to perform it presents itself.", :sources ["Leviticus 22:30-31" "Pesachim 3a" "Sukkah 25a" "Yoma 33a" "Bava Kamma 38a"]}}], :arithmetic [{:kind :other, :note "Semikhut parshiyot (adjacency): the verse about leaving over is juxtaposed with the verse about keeping commandments", :by :selah, :ran? false}], :book :leviticus, :voice :baal-haturim, :lang :en, :chapter 22, :discipline "bht-chalkboard-v1", :timestamp "2026-07-12T23:25:04.346207063Z", :model "glm-5.2"}